    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"MultiObjective_Transportation\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    I = 5  # sources\n    J = 5  # destinations\n    K = 3  # max suppliers per store\n    w_cost = 0.6\n    w_emission = 0.4\n\n    supplies = [186, 212, 127, 187, 222]\n    demands = [59, 55, 55, 55, 21]\n    cost_matrix = [[11.01, 6.7, 17.3, 6.21, 29.71], [13.78, 27.4, 9.99, 3.61, 11.54], [9.38, 20.99, 28.52, 9.25, 17.25], [3.87, 23.6, 26.49, 3.54, 20.34], [9.34, 8.53, 10.0, 22.53, 12.16]]\n    emission_matrix = [[18.69, 11.41, 11.42, 6.39, 4.04], [8.45, 7.27, 14.23, 14.06, 16.42], [20.0, 12.07, 7.5, 4.64, 12.16], [19.65, 9.69, 1.0, 12.31, 16.76], [13.7, 14.67, 14.09, 1.94, 12.26]]\n    fixed_costs = [[274, 229, 78, 202, 82], [232, 229, 242, 192, 65], [290, 130, 210, 113, 281], [247, 230, 82, 59, 149], [249, 162, 278, 162, 283]]\n    moq = [[15, 18, 16, 7, 6], [10, 19, 13, 8, 15], [7, 14, 8, 7, 10], [5, 12, 8, 15, 6], [6, 11, 8, 13, 17]]\n\n    # Big-M per route\n    total_demand = sum(demands)\n    big_M = [[min(supplies[i], total_demand) for j in range(J)] for i in range(I)]\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Continuous: shipment quantities\n    x = {}\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            x[(i,j)] = m.addVar(lb=0, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Ship_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # Binary: route-open indicators\n    y = {}\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            y[(i,j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"RouteOpen_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # --- Objective ---\n    # Weighted variable cost + fixed setup costs\n    m.setObjective(\n        quicksum(\n            (w_cost * cost_matrix[i][j] + w_emission * emission_matrix[i][j]) * x[(i,j)]\n            for i in range(I) for j in range(J)\n        )\n        + quicksum(fixed_costs[i][j] * y[(i,j)] for i in range(I) for j in range(J)),\n        GRB.MINIMIZE\n    )\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # C1: Supply\n    for i in range(I):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(x[(i,j)] for j in range(J)) <= supplies[i],\n            name=f\"Supply_S{i}\")\n\n    # C2: Demand\n    for j in range(J):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(x[(i,j)] for i in range(I)) >= demands[j],\n            name=f\"Demand_D{j}\")\n\n    # C3: Linking \u2014 can only ship if route is open\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            m.addConstr(\n                x[(i,j)] <= big_M[i][j] * y[(i,j)],\n                name=f\"Link_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # C4: Minimum order quantity\n    for i in range(I):\n        for j in range(J):\n            m.addConstr(\n                x[(i,j)] >= moq[i][j] * y[(i,j)],\n                name=f\"MOQ_S{i}_D{j}\")\n\n    # C5: Max suppliers per store\n    for j in range(J):\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(y[(i,j)] for i in range(I)) <= K,\n            name=f\"MaxSupplier_D{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "To: Executive Supply Chain Committee\nFrom: Sustainability & Logistics Director\nSubject: Balancing shipping cost and carbon while respecting real-world operating rules\nDate: Current\n\nContext\nOur network ships product from multiple warehouses to multiple stores. Each lane is evaluated on both per-unit shipping spend and per-unit carbon emissions. In practice, the lowest-cost lanes often carry higher emissions, creating a clear trade-off between margin discipline and our sustainability commitments. We need a pragmatic plan that respects operational realities while balancing these priorities.\n\nOperational rules we must observe\n- Route activation fees: Starting shipments on any lane triggers an upfront activation expense (contracting, compliance, dock staging). This charge applies whenever the lane is used, independent of the shipment volume on that lane.\n- Minimum order quantities: Once a lane is opened, we must move at least the prescribed minimum on that lane to justify dock time and handling resources; small partial loads are not accepted.\n- Supplier limits per store: To manage receiving capacity, vendor administration, and contractual complexity, each store may be supplied by no more than a capped number of warehouses.\n\nSupply and demand boundaries\n- Warehouse supply: Each warehouse has finite available units for outbound shipment; our plan will not assign more than what is available at any origin.\n- Store demand: Each store has a required minimum that must be met in full; shortfalls are not acceptable.\n\nManagement priority: cost versus emissions\nLeadership has set the relative importance of cost and emissions. We will evaluate plans using a weighted combination of total variable shipping spend and total emissions. Route activation fees will be added in full when a lane is used; only the per-unit shipping spend and per-unit emissions are blended using the management weights.\n\nGoal\nIdentify which lanes to activate and how much to move on each active lane so that:\n- warehouse inventories are respected\n- every store\u2019s minimum demand is met\n- every active lane carries at least its minimum order\n- each store receives from no more than the allowed number of warehouses\n- the weighted combination of total variable cost and total emissions, plus all lane activation fees, is kept as low as practical under these rules\n\nNext steps\nThe analytics team will populate the data elements in the annex and run scenario tests under the stated priorities. We will return with a recommended activation list and shipment plan, including a clear summary of budget impact, carbon impact, and sensitivity to the cost-versus-emissions weights.\n\nAnnex \u2014 Data (to be populated by Analytics)\n- NUM_SOURCES: 5\n- NUM_DESTINATIONS: 5\n- SUPPLIES: Warehouse capacities: [186, 212, 127, 187, 222]\n- DEMANDS: Store demand requirements: [59, 55, 55, 55, 21]\n- COST_MATRIX: Shipping Costs ($/unit, 5 sources x 5 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4\n------------------------------------\nS0         |  11.01 |   6.70 |  17.30 |   6.21 |  29.71\nS1         |  13.78 |  27.40 |   9.99 |   3.61 |  11.54\nS2         |   9.38 |  20.99 |  28.52 |   9.25 |  17.25\nS3         |   3.87 |  23.60 |  26.49 |   3.54 |  20.34\nS4         |   9.34 |   8.53 |  10.00 |  22.53 |  12.16\n- EMISSION_MATRIX: CO2 Emissions (kg CO2/unit, 5 sources x 5 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4\n------------------------------------\nS0         |  18.69 |  11.41 |  11.42 |   6.39 |   4.04\nS1         |   8.45 |   7.27 |  14.23 |  14.06 |  16.42\nS2         |  20.00 |  12.07 |   7.50 |   4.64 |  12.16\nS3         |  19.65 |   9.69 |   1.00 |  12.31 |  16.76\nS4         |  13.70 |  14.67 |  14.09 |   1.94 |  12.26\n- WEIGHT_COST: 60%\n- WEIGHT_EMISSION: 40%\n- FIXED_COSTS: Route Setup Costs ($, 5 sources x 5 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4\n------------------------------------\nS0         |    274 |    229 |     78 |    202 |     82\nS1         |    232 |    229 |    242 |    192 |     65\nS2         |    290 |    130 |    210 |    113 |    281\nS3         |    247 |    230 |     82 |     59 |    149\nS4         |    249 |    162 |    278 |    162 |    283\n- MIN_ORDER_QUANTITIES: Minimum Order Quantities (units, 5 sources x 5 destinations):\nSource\\Dest | D0 | D1 | D2 | D3 | D4\n------------------------------------\nS0         |     15 |     18 |     16 |      7 |      6\nS1         |     10 |     19 |     13 |      8 |     15\nS2         |      7 |     14 |      8 |      7 |     10\nS3         |      5 |     12 |      8 |     15 |      6\nS4         |      6 |     11 |      8 |     13 |     17\n- MAX_SUPPLIERS_PER_STORE: 3",
    "LLM_description_template": "To: Executive Supply Chain Committee\nFrom: Sustainability & Logistics Director\nSubject: Balancing shipping cost and carbon while respecting real-world operating rules\nDate: Current\n\nContext\nOur network ships product from multiple warehouses to multiple stores. Each lane is evaluated on both per-unit shipping spend and per-unit carbon emissions. In practice, the lowest-cost lanes often carry higher emissions, creating a clear trade-off between margin discipline and our sustainability commitments. We need a pragmatic plan that respects operational realities while balancing these priorities.\n\nOperational rules we must observe\n- Route activation fees: Starting shipments on any lane triggers an upfront activation expense (contracting, compliance, dock staging). This charge applies whenever the lane is used, independent of the shipment volume on that lane.\n- Minimum order quantities: Once a lane is opened, we must move at least the prescribed minimum on that lane to justify dock time and handling resources; small partial loads are not accepted.\n- Supplier limits per store: To manage receiving capacity, vendor administration, and contractual complexity, each store may be supplied by no more than a capped number of warehouses.\n\nSupply and demand boundaries\n- Warehouse supply: Each warehouse has finite available units for outbound shipment; our plan will not assign more than what is available at any origin.\n- Store demand: Each store has a required minimum that must be met in full; shortfalls are not acceptable.\n\nManagement priority: cost versus emissions\nLeadership has set the relative importance of cost and emissions. We will evaluate plans using a weighted combination of total variable shipping spend and total emissions. Route activation fees will be added in full when a lane is used; only the per-unit shipping spend and per-unit emissions are blended using the management weights.\n\nGoal\nIdentify which lanes to activate